  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam International</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam International will be working on the intersection of climate and inequality, producing research reports and run campaigns to create awareness and shift the terms of debate. The second most important thing that needs to happen is to ensure that climate finance is provided to developing countries. Without climate finance, there can be no climate action. Communities at the frontline of theclimate crisis cannot adapt, recover, and rebuild their lives after a disaster if there is no finance available to them. Oxfam willcontinue to track the delivery of finance to developing countries, especially to local communities, by conducting action
research at local levels to see whether finance is reaching those most in need. We will use the outcomes of the research to influence UNFCCC meetings on climate finance. We will also use the outcomes of this research, as well as other findings, to influence G7, G20 meetings, as well as the WB/IMF meetings &#x2013; asking developed countries to meet their commitment to deliver $100 billion per year to developing countries, especially to local communities. By combining research, advocacy, media, and campaigns work, we plan keep rich countries accountable for reducing emissions and providing finance to developing countries; weplan to ensure that finance in the form of grants reaches communities. On land, will conduct advocacy and lobby meetings to strengthen and accelerate the implementation of the international frameworks and commitments towards securing land rights (such asSDGs, FPIC, VGGTs). Support communities and CSOs to demand the recognition and protection of their land and territorial rights. (eg. Through Land Rights Now or supporting a country level advocacy, eviction cases etc.). Prepare research reports and build evidence bases to support national and global advocacy related to land rights; Empower communities and CSOs to hold governments to account through engagement in international human rights and other mechanisms (shadow reporting for CEDAW, ICESCR, SDGs etc).</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org ref="NL-KVK-27108436" role="4" type="21">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam International was formed in 1995 by a group of independent non-governmental organizations. Their aim was to work together forgreater impact on the international stage to reduce poverty and injustice.
The name &quot;Oxfam&quot; comes from the Oxford Committee for Famine Relief, founded in Britain in 1942. The group campaigned for food supplies to be sent through an allied naval blockade to starving women and children in enemy-occupied Greece during the Second World War.
As well as becoming a world leader in the delivery of emergency relief, Oxfam International implements long-term development programs in vulnerable communities. We are also part of a global movement, campaigning with others, for instance, to end unfair trade rules, demand better health and education services for all, and to combat climate change.
Today, there are 19 member organizations of the Oxfam International confederation. They are based in: Australia, Belgium, Canada, Denmark, France, Germany, Great Britain, Hong Kong, Ireland, India, Italy, Japan, Mexico, The Netherlands, New Zealand, Quebec, South Africa, Spain and the United States.
The Oxfam International Secretariat is based in Oxford, UK. The Secretariat runs advocacy offices in Addis Ababa, Brussels, Geneva, New York and Washington DC.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam Novib</narrative>
